INSERT INTO feature VALUES('abi-realizability','runtime','Layouts as realizability predicates','foreign-calls','partial','uncommon','mainstream languages','A type''s realisers are a predicate on a word and a heap written as data, the separating conjunction a sigma over a disjoint split and points-to a singleton, so the normaliser computes every assertion and the kernel replays it (Wagner, Eisbach and Ahmed, OOPSLA 2024): `Option<&T>`''s null niche is equivalent at every heap to the two-word tagged layout, its marshallers meet their triples, and a C `deref_or` specified by hand is met by it, its `_Nonnull` neighbour refused. A Rust extern''s function row, `std.boundary`, reads each argument''s and the result''s witness record off its code by Rust''s default layout and is admitted against the signature rustc printed by a run, a differing signature unrealised by the clause it breaks: size, alignment, niche, drop or arity. An `extern "rust"` block naming its members'' rows is admitted where it is declared, by a run of `admitBlock` over the codes of its fixed-width atoms. `deref_or` runs on the word a Rust caller passes for an `Option<&u32>`, the null word answering its default and any other the `u32` the caller''s host reads there, as Rust''s own does. The logic is not yet a theory, and the C side''s specification is assumed until a foreign declaration lowers.',NULL,'library/std/src/boundary.gandr corpus/model/specification/niche-realizability.gandr corpus/pathological/specification/niche-realizability.gandr corpus/model/interop/boundary/function-row.gandr corpus/pathological/interop/boundary/function-row.gandr corpus/model/interop/boundary/round-trip.gandr corpus/model/interop/boundary/by-layout.gandr','corpus/ledger/features/abi-realizability.gandr','#2835 #327');
